-- title:
--   Rank of a cohomologically trivial lattice over a p-group
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a commutative ring which is a local principal ideal domain, let $p$ be a prime and assume the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$ has characteristic $p$. Let $G$ be a finite group that is a $p$-group, and let $P$ be an $\mathcal{O}$-module which is finite (finitely generated) and torsion-free, carrying an $\mathcal{O}$-linear action of $G$ given by a representation $\rho$. Two hypotheses are imposed on $\rho$: first, every $w \in P$ fixed by all $\rho(g)$, $g \in G$, lies in the image of the norm endomorphism $\rho.norm = \sum_{g \in G} \rho(g)$, i.e. $w = \rho.norm(m)$ for some $m \in P$; second, every $v \in P$ with $\rho.norm(v) = 0$ lies in `Representation.Coinvariants.ker ρ`, the submodule generated by the elements $\rho(g)x - x$. Equivalently, the Tate cohomology groups $\hat H^{0}(G,P)$ and $\hat H^{-1}(G,P)$ vanish. The conclusion is the equality of $\mathcal{O}$-ranks $$\operatorname{finrank}_{\mathcal{O}} P = |G| \cdot \operatorname{finrank}_{\mathcal{O}} \big(\operatorname{Coinvariants} \rho\big),$$ where $\operatorname{Coinvariants} \rho$ is the quotient of $P$ by that submodule.
--
--   This is the numerical content of Nakayama's criterion for cohomological triviality: vanishing of two consecutive Tate cohomology groups forces the lattice $P$ to be free over $\mathcal{O}[G]$ of rank $\operatorname{rank}_{\mathcal{O}} P_G$, whence the stated rank identity. It is used in the Taylor–Wiles argument, in the computation of the rank of a corner submodule of a first cohomology group in [`CohCarrier.finrank_cornerSubmodule_H1_eq_relIndex_mul_of_not_isEisenstein_subfamily`](thm.html#CohCarrier.finrank_cornerSubmodule_H1_eq_relIndex_mul_of_not_isEisenstein_subfamily). The proof cites [`Representation.exists_ne_zero_forall_apply_eq_of_isPGroup`](thm.html#Representation.exists_ne_zero_forall_apply_eq_of_isPGroup), the existence of a nonzero invariant vector for a representation of a finite $p$-group on a nonzero vector space over a field of characteristic $p$.
